from engine import Author
A=Author('17');add=A.add
add('4.2.1-01','A luminous object emits its own visible light.','An illuminated object is seen by light received from another source and redirected toward the eye.','A switched-on lamp is luminous, while a book beside it is illuminated. The Moon is mainly seen by reflected sunlight rather than producing its own visible light. Light must enter the eye for either to be seen. Avoid describing ordinary vision as rays leaving the eye to touch the object.','Explain the difference between luminous and illuminated objects.','A luminous object emits its own light.~An illuminated object is seen using light received from another source.')
add('4.2.1-02','In a uniform medium, the ray model represents light travelling along straight lines.','Blocking or selecting these paths explains shadows, pinhole images and eclipses.','A ray traces a propagation direction rather than a physical thin rod. Use boundary rays from the source edges to identify the illuminated and shadow regions. Refraction and reflection change direction at interfaces; they do not contradict straight propagation between interactions.','Explain the common ray-model link between a shadow and a pinhole image.','Link both to straight propagation in a uniform medium.~An obstruction removes certain paths, while a pinhole selects narrow paths reaching the screen.')
add('4.2.1-02-M01','An opaque object can block direct light paths to form a shadow.','An extended source can form an umbra with no direct source light and a penumbra receiving light from part of the source.','Draw rays from both edges of the source past the edges of the obstacle. Where all direct source rays are blocked, the umbra forms. Where only some are blocked, the penumbra forms. A point-source approximation gives a sharper boundary than a broad source. Scattered room light can make a real shadow less dark without changing which direct paths are blocked.','Explain why a broad lamp can produce a partially dark shadow edge.','Some of the extended source is blocked while another part remains visible from that region.~It receives partial illumination and forms a penumbra.')
add('4.2.1-02-M02','A pinhole selects narrow straight-line paths from an object to a screen.','The resulting image is inverted because rays from opposite object regions cross at the opening.','A ray from the object top passes through the hole to the lower screen; one from the bottom reaches the upper screen. A smaller hole reduces overlap and can sharpen the image, but also reduces brightness until other effects become important. A larger screen distance can enlarge the image while spreading the admitted light over more area.','Explain why a pinhole camera forms an inverted image.','Light from the top travels through the hole to the bottom of the screen.~Light from the bottom reaches the upper screen, so the image is inverted.')
add('4.2.1-02-M03','A solar eclipse occurs when the Moon lies between Sun and Earth and its shadow reaches Earth.','A lunar eclipse occurs when Earth lies between Sun and Moon and the Moon enters Earth’s shadow.','The umbra corresponds to complete blocking of direct sunlight and the penumbra to partial blocking. The required alignment does not happen at every lunar cycle because the orbital planes differ. In a diagram, place the light source first and trace the outermost rays. Direct observation of the Sun requires appropriate specialist protection; the model can be studied using safe lamps and balls.','Explain the difference between a solar and lunar eclipse using shadows.','A solar eclipse places part of Earth in the Moon’s shadow.~A lunar eclipse places the Moon in Earth’s shadow, with umbra and penumbra describing complete or partial direct-light blocking.')
add('4.3.1-01','Transparent, translucent and opaque describe different transmission behaviour.','Reflection and absorption may accompany transmission at a surface.','A ray box, test sheet and screen let you compare how much light passes and whether it stays sufficiently directed to show a clear image. Use matched lighting and distances. A transparent sample transmits a clear view; a translucent sample scatters transmitted light; an opaque sample has negligible visible transmission in the stated thickness.','Explain how a ray-box test can distinguish transparent, translucent and opaque samples.','Combine transmission and image clarity: transparent material passes a clear directed view, while translucent material passes scattered light.~Opaque material blocks appreciable transmission to the screen.')
add('4.3.1-01-M01','A transparent material transmits light with enough direction preserved to see a clear image.','Some reflection or absorption can still occur.','Place clear glass or Perspex in a narrow ray path and observe the transmitted ray and a target viewed through it. The ray may change direction at boundaries, so transparent does not mean no optical effect. Use safe low-intensity light and do not stare into a beam.','Describe a test for transparency.','Check that the sample transmits light and permits a clear view of a target through it.')
add('4.3.1-01-M02','A translucent material transmits light but scatters it strongly enough to obscure a clear view.','Its transmitted screen patch can be broader or more diffuse than a clear sample’s.','Compare tracing paper with a clear sheet under the same illumination. A target may disappear as a distinct image even though light still passes. Do not classify it as opaque merely because a sharp ray cannot be followed through it.','Describe how to identify a translucent sample.','Show that light passes through but a target is blurred or not clearly resolved because the transmitted light is scattered.')
add('4.3.1-01-M03','An opaque material transmits negligible visible light through its stated thickness.','Incident light can instead be reflected or absorbed.','Put card in the ray path and compare the screen behind it with an unobstructed reference. A shadow indicates blocked direct transmission. A mirror is opaque despite reflecting strongly, so opacity is not the same as blackness or total absorption.','Describe a test for opacity.','Place the sample in a light path and check that appreciable direct visible light does not pass through it.')
add('4.3.2-01','A plane-mirror image is located where backward extensions of reflected rays meet.','The light itself remains in front of the reflecting surface in this construction.','For a vertical mirror at x = 0 and a point at (−2, 1), the image is at (2, 1) in arbitrary length units. Draw rays to two separated mirror points, reflect them using equal normal angles, then extend the outgoing rays backward using dashed lines. Dashed extensions distinguish the virtual construction from real propagation.','Draw a plane-mirror image construction for a point 2 cm in front of the mirror using two reflected rays.','Draw two rays from the point to the mirror and reflect each using equal normal angles.~Extend reflected rays backward as dashed lines to meet at the virtual point 2 cm behind the mirror.')
segs=[dict(points=[[0,-2],[0,3]],color='black')]
for y in [-1,2]:
 segs += [dict(points=[[-2,1],[0,y]],arrow=True),dict(points=[[0,y],[-2,2*y-1]],arrow=True),dict(points=[[0,y],[2,1]],style='--',color='tab:orange')]
A.geometry('4.3.2-01',segs,labels=[[-2.4,1.2,'Object'],[1.7,1.2,'Virtual image'],[.1,2.8,'Mirror']],title='Plane-mirror point construction')
add('4.3.2-02','A plane mirror gives an upright virtual image of the same size as the object.','Its image is the same perpendicular distance behind the mirror as the object is in front, with lateral inversion in the syllabus description.','Position is measured perpendicular to the mirror, not along a slanting eye-to-image line. The image is virtual because the reflected rays do not actually meet behind the mirror. The familiar left-right appearance arises from front-back reversal and how an object is compared with its reflected orientation.','Explain how ray construction accounts for a plane mirror’s virtual image position.','Link equal reflection angles to backward ray extensions meeting equally far behind the surface.~The meeting is virtual because the actual reflected light does not pass through it.')
add('4.3.2-02-M01','A plane-mirror image is virtual and lies as far behind the mirror as the object is in front.','This is a perpendicular distance comparison.','An object 15 cm in front has an image 15 cm behind, giving an object-image separation of 30 cm. A screen behind an opaque mirror cannot collect that virtual image because the reflected rays do not converge there.','State the nature of a plane-mirror image.','It is virtual.')
add('4.3.2-02-M02','A plane-mirror image is upright and the same size as its object.','It shows lateral inversion in the usual reflected-view description.','A written symbol can reveal the changed orientation. The mirror does not magnify merely because it is larger; a larger mirror can admit rays from a greater field without changing the image-size ratio. Keep image size distinct from how much of the image is visible.','State the image-size comparison for an ideal plane mirror.','The image is the same size as the object.')
add('4.3.2-03','The normal is perpendicular to the reflecting surface at the ray’s incidence point.','Incidence and reflection angles are measured between their rays and this normal.','A ray 25° to the surface is 65° to the normal. Read a protractor from the correct baseline and mark the incidence point before drawing the normal. Confusing the surface with the normal can give complementary angles that are consistently wrong despite a neat diagram.','State the reference line from which incidence angle is measured.','The normal at the point where the ray strikes the surface.')
add('4.3.2-04','Reflection can provide useful viewing or light direction but can also cause glare.','Mirror shape affects whether rays remain spread, converge or diverge.','A plane mirror provides a familiar undistorted reflected view. A convex mirror offers a wider field with smaller images, useful for surveillance; a concave reflector can direct light when a source is suitably placed. These are qualitative applications, not a new curved-mirror calculation topic. Unwanted reflected light from a bright surface can reduce visibility.','Explain one benefit and one drawback of reflection in everyday viewing.','A mirror can redirect light to show a region outside direct view.~Unwanted reflection from a bright surface can produce glare and reduce visibility.')
add('4.3.2-05','For reflection at a plane surface, angle of incidence equals angle of reflection.','Both angles use the same normal.','If the incident ray is 38° to the normal, the reflected ray is also 38° to the normal. The angle between the two rays in front of the mirror is then 76°. A normal-incidence ray retraces its path; do not measure its 90° surface angle as the incidence angle.','Calculate the reflection angle when the incident ray is 32° to the normal.','The law is r = i.~The reflection angle is 32°.')
add('4.3.2-06','Ray constructions require a labelled surface, incidence point, normal and directional rays.','Geometry can relate surface angles, normal angles and the separation of rays.','For an incident ray 40° to a horizontal mirror surface, incidence is 50° to the vertical normal. Draw the reflected ray at the same 50° normal angle on the other side. Its surface angle is again 40° and the two rays enclose 100° in front of the surface. Arrowheads show propagation toward and away from the mirror.','Draw a reflected-ray construction for an incident ray 40° to a horizontal mirror surface, marking the normal angles.','Draw the perpendicular normal at incidence and label i = 50°.~Draw the outgoing ray on the opposite side of the normal at r = 50°, with appropriate arrowheads.')
v=2.5;dx=v/math.tan(math.radians(40))
A.geometry('4.3.2-06',[dict(points=[[-4,0],[4,0]],color='black'),dict(points=[[0,0],[0,3]],style='--',color='grey'),dict(points=[[-dx,v],[0,0]],arrow=True),dict(points=[[0,0],[dx,v]],arrow=True)],labels=[[-1.3,1.3,'i = 50°'],[.3,1.3,'r = 50°'],[-3,-.4,'Mirror']],title='Angles measured from the normal')
