C('4.2.1-01','Which is normally seen mainly by reflected sunlight?',('The Moon','It is illuminated by the Sun.'),('The Sun','It emits visible light.'),('A glowing lamp filament','It emits light.'),('A flame','It emits light.'))
C('4.2.1-02-M01','What is a penumbra?',('A partially illuminated shadow region','Only part of the source is blocked.'),('A region with every direct source ray blocked','That is umbra.'),('A mirror image','This is a different optical effect.'),('A light source','It is a shadow region.'))
C('4.2.1-02-M02','What is the usual pinhole-image orientation?',('Inverted','Paths cross at the opening.'),('Upright due to reflection','No mirror is required.'),('Determined by the screen’s colour alone','Geometry sets the orientation.'),('No image can form using a small opening alone','A pinhole can select paths.'))
C('4.3.1-01-M02','Which description fits tracing paper?',('Transmits scattered light','It is a typical translucent material.'),('Transmits a clear view like an ideal clear sheet','Scattering obscures the view.'),('Emits its own light necessarily','Illumination does not imply emission.'),('Reflects all light with no transmission','It can transmit light.'))
C('4.3.2-02-M01','What is the nature of a plane-mirror image?',('Virtual','Backward extensions meet, not actual reflected rays.'),('Real on a screen behind the mirror','Rays do not converge there.'),('Necessarily inverted top to bottom','The ordinary image is upright.'),('Larger than the object','Its ideal size equals the object.'))
C('4.3.2-03','What is the normal?',('A perpendicular to the surface at incidence','It sets angle references.'),('A line along the surface','That is the surface direction.'),('The source-to-eye distance','That is unrelated to the definition.'),('The image height','That is a different measure.'))
C('4.3.2-04','Which qualitative benefit can a convex viewing mirror provide?',('A wider field of view','Its smaller images show a broader region.'),('Larger undistorted images at every position','That is not its general viewing effect.'),('Light creation','It reflects incoming light.'),('Complete removal of glare everywhere','Reflection can also create glare.'))
C('4.3.2-05','Incidence is 28° to the normal. What is reflection angle?',('28°','The two normal angles are equal.'),('62°','That is the complementary surface angle.'),('56°','That is the ray separation in this geometry.'),('14°','There is no halving relation.'))
C('4.3.2-06','An incident ray is 20° to a mirror surface. What is its normal angle?',('70°','The normal is 90° to the surface.'),('20°','This repeats the surface angle.'),('40°','Doubling is not the conversion.'),('110°','Use the acute angle to the normal here.'))
C('4.3.2-02-M01','An object is 12 cm in front of a plane mirror. Where is its image?',('12 cm behind','Perpendicular distances match.'),('6 cm behind','The distance does not halve.'),('24 cm behind','That confuses separation with image distance.'),('On the front surface','The virtual image lies behind.'))
C('4.2.1-02-M03','Which alignment gives a lunar eclipse?',('Sun–Earth–Moon','Earth’s shadow falls on the Moon.'),('Sun–Moon–Earth','This is the solar-eclipse alignment.'),('Earth–Sun–Moon','Earth does not lie between source and Moon.'),('Moon–Sun–Earth','The Moon is not in Earth’s shadow in this alignment.'))
C('4.3.2-05','Incidence is 35° to the normal. What angle lies between the incident and reflected ray lines in front of the mirror?',('70°','The two normal angles add.'),('35°','This gives one normal angle only.'),('55°','This gives one surface angle.'),('140°','This doubles the already combined angle.'))
P=A.part
